const e=[{id:"sv-1-411",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:`A radio-network Green Belt categorises one month of **dropped-call** tickets and produces the Pareto chart below.

Based on the chart, which categories form the **vital few** that together account for roughly 80% or more of the tickets?`,options:["Coverage only (41.2%)","Coverage and Handover (69.9% cumulative)","Coverage, Handover and Congestion (87.5% cumulative)","All categories except Other (97.4% cumulative)"],answer:2,hint:"Read the Cum % row (or the red cumulative line) from left to right and stop at the first bar where it passes about 80%.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Use the **Cum %** row under the bars: 41.2 → 69.9 → 87.5 → 93.3 …"},{title:"What it shows",body:"The cumulative share first passes 80% at the **third** bar (87.5%). The remaining bars together add only 12.5%."},{title:"Decide",body:"The vital few are **Coverage, Handover and Congestion** → answer **C**."}],final:"Three bars reach 87.5% cumulative → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Coverage alone is only 41.2% — well short of the ~80% the vital few should cover.","Two bars reach only 69.9%; the cumulative line passes 80% at the third bar.","","Including Interference and Hardware adds the trivial many — they add only a few percent each."],trap:"The 80:20 rule is a guide, not a law — pick the fewest bars that reach about 80%, not exactly 20% of the categories.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Dropped-Call Cause",categories:[{name:"Coverage",count:412},{name:"Handover",count:287},{name:"Congestion",count:176},{name:"Interferen",count:58},{name:"Hardware",count:41},{name:"Other",count:26}],yLabel:"Tickets"},bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-412",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"A customer-care team plots last quarter’s written complaints by reason. Which statement about this Pareto chart is TRUE?",options:["Billing and Activation are clearly the vital few, so the remaining categories can be ignored","“Other” is drawn last because it is the smallest category","The chart shows that complaints are spread evenly, so Pareto analysis is not useful here","“Other” holds 27.4% of complaints, so it should be broken into more specific categories before the vital few are chosen"],answer:3,hint:"Check the size of the “Other” bar. When it is large, the categories are too broad (or too many items were lumped together).",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Look at the last bar, **Other**: 236 complaints, 27.4% of the total."},{title:"What it shows",body:"“Other” is bigger than every named bar except Billing. A good Pareto keeps “Other” small (often under 5–10%)."},{title:"Decide",body:"Re-code the “Other” complaints into specific reasons, then rebuild the chart → answer **D**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“Other” is always plotted last by convention — not because it is smallest."}],final:"“Other” = 27.4% → re-categorise first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Billing + Activation reach only 49.1%; with “Other” at 27.4% the chart cannot yet separate the vital few.","Minitab always draws “Other” last whatever its size — here it is the second-largest group (27.4%).","The named bars fall steeply (268 → 44); the problem is the oversized “Other” bar, not an even spread.",""],trap:"A large “Other” bar hides the real vital few.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Complaint Reason",categories:[{name:"Billing",count:268},{name:"Activation",count:154},{name:"Porting",count:97},{name:"Roaming",count:61},{name:"Handset",count:44},{name:"Other",count:236}],yLabel:"Complaints"},bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-413",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt charts the reasons enterprise orders are returned for correction. Based on the chart, what should the team do next?",options:["Stratify the data another way (for example by sales channel or by cost per error), because no clear vital few appears","Work on Invoice errors only, because it is the tallest bar","Conclude that Pareto analysis proves all causes are equally serious and stop the project","Work on the first two bars because they reach 80%"],answer:0,hint:"A useful Pareto has a few tall bars and a steep cumulative line. A flat chart means the data should be cut another way.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Compare the bar heights (64, 61, 59, 57, 55, 52) and the Cum % row."},{title:"What it shows",body:"The bars are almost equal and the cumulative line rises nearly straight; 80% needs 5 of 7 categories."},{title:"Decide",body:"There is no Pareto effect in this view. Re-stratify (channel, region, cost) or weight by impact to find a vital few → answer **A**."}],final:"Flat Pareto → re-stratify the data → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Invoice is only 17.8% — fixing it alone would remove fewer than one return in five.","A flat chart means this stratification does not separate the causes; it does not prove anything about seriousness or end the project.","The first two bars reach only 34.7%; 80% is not passed until the 5th bar."],trap:"A flat Pareto is still information: this way of grouping the data does not reveal a vital few.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Order Return Reason",categories:[{name:"Invoice",count:64},{name:"Contract",count:61},{name:"Address",count:59},{name:"Tariff",count:57},{name:"Discount",count:55},{name:"Tax code",count:52},{name:"Other",count:12}],yLabel:"Returned orders"},bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-414",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:`Ranked by **count**, the most frequent home-broadband failure is “Callback” (customer has to call again), followed by “SIM swap”. The Green Belt then rebuilds the Pareto using the **cost of poor quality** of each failure type, shown below.

The project aims to cut COPQ. Which failure type should the team target first?`,options:["Truck roll — it carries 45.3% of the failure cost","Callback — it is the most frequent failure","SIM swap — it is second by count and appears in both charts","Other — it collects the many small costs that add up"],answer:0,hint:"When the goal is money, rank by cost (or cost × count), not by number of occurrences.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Read the y-axis label — this Pareto is in **OMR**, not in counts."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Truck roll is the tallest bar (45.3% of COPQ); with Credit (service credits) it reaches 71.3%."},{title:"Decide",body:"A COPQ goal is served by attacking the costliest failure first → Truck roll → answer **A**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The count Pareto answers “what happens most often”; the cost Pareto answers “what hurts the business most”."}],final:"Truck roll = 45.3% of COPQ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Frequency is not cost: on the cost chart Callback is only 4.3% of COPQ.","SIM swap is just 5.8% of the cost; appearing in both charts does not make it the priority.","“Other” is the smallest share here (2.3%) and is always drawn last."],trap:"Always check what the Pareto y-axis measures: counts and costs can give different vital few.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Failure Cost (OMR) by Type",categories:[{name:"Truck roll",count:41200},{name:"Credit",count:23600},{name:"Re-install",count:14800},{name:"SIM swap",count:5300},{name:"Callback",count:3900},{name:"Other",count:2100}],yLabel:"Cost (OMR)"},bookRef:"Book pp. 585, 588"},{id:"sv-1-415",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:"Detractor comments from a contact-centre survey are coded and charted. Based on the chart, what share of detractor comments is explained by the **two** largest categories?",options:["44.4%","72.9%","28.6%","86.3%"],answer:1,hint:"For “the top k categories together”, read the Cum % value under the k-th bar.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Find the **Cum %** row under the second bar (Transfer)."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Cum % after two bars = 72.9% (Wait time 44.4% + Transfer 28.6%)."},{title:"Decide",body:"The two largest categories explain 72.9% → answer **B**."}],final:"Cum % at bar 2 = 72.9% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["This is the first bar only (Wait time); the question asks for the first two.","","This is the Percent of the second bar alone, not the cumulative share of both.","This is the cumulative percent after **three** bars."],figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Detractor Comment Theme",categories:[{name:"Wait time",count:318},{name:"Transfer",count:205},{name:"Wrong info",count:96},{name:"Rude agent",count:47},{name:"Language",count:29},{name:"Other",count:22}],yLabel:"Comments"},bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-416",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"The chart shows billing-error tickets for one month. Which statement about the chart is NOT correct?",options:["The left axis shows the count of tickets and the right axis shows cumulative percent","Proration and Duplicate charges together account for 66.2% of the tickets","The chart proves that proration logic is the root cause of the billing errors","“Other” is placed last even though it is a combined group"],answer:2,hint:"A Pareto chart ranks categories by frequency. It shows where problems cluster, not why they happen.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Check each statement against the axes, the Cum % row and the bar order."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Counts on the left, percent on the right; Cum % at bar 2 = 66.2%; “Other” is last. All of these are visible."},{title:"Decide",body:"Ranking shows **where** to focus. Proving the **root cause** needs Analyze-phase tools (fishbone, 5 Whys, hypothesis tests) → the NOT-correct statement is **C**."}],final:"Pareto ≠ root-cause proof → answer **C**",whyWrong:["True — Minitab’s Pareto puts counts on the left axis and cumulative percent on the right.","True — the Cum % under the second bar is 66.2%.","","True — “Other” is always drawn last by convention."],trap:"Pareto tells you which category to dig into; it does not tell you why it happens.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Billing Error Type",categories:[{name:"Proration",count:230},{name:"Duplicate",count:141},{name:"Late fee",count:88},{name:"Discount",count:52},{name:"Tax",count:30},{name:"Other",count:19}],yLabel:"Tickets"},bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-417",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:`A Champion asks for **one** focused Green Belt project to cut escalations to the CEO office. The chart shows last half-year’s escalations by service area (“Fibre inst” = fibre installation, “Fault rep” = fault repair).

Which project scope does the chart best support?`,options:["All escalations across every service area","Fault repair and bill queries, because they are two different teams","Relocation, because it is the easiest to fix","Fibre installation escalations, which make up 52.0% of the total"],answer:3,hint:"A good scope is narrow enough to manage but covers a large share of the problem — look for a single dominant bar.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Compare the first bar with the rest."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Fibre installation alone gives 52.0% of escalations — more than all other areas combined."},{title:"Decide",body:"Scope the charter to fibre installation escalations → answer **D**."}],final:"One bar = 52.0% → scope on Fibre installation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Scope too wide for one Green Belt project; the chart already shows one dominant area.","Together they are only 31.3% — far less than Fibre installation alone.","Ease of fixing cannot be read from the chart; Relocation is only 9.3% of escalations.",""],figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of CEO Escalations by Service Area",categories:[{name:"Fibre inst",count:486},{name:"Fault rep",count:172},{name:"Bill query",count:121},{name:"Relocation",count:87},{name:"Upgrade",count:48},{name:"Other",count:21}],yLabel:"Escalations"},bookRef:"Book pp. 230, 588"},{id:"sv-1-418",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:`A home-internet team codes 771 open-ended survey answers to the question “What would make you recommend us?” The coded **Voice of the Customer** is charted below.

Which requirement should the team translate into a CTQ first?`,options:["Monthly price compared with competitors","Delivered broadband speed (for example, % of speed tests at or above the plan speed)","Mobile-app rating in the app store","Number of complaints answered within the SLA"],answer:1,hint:"Start with the theme customers raise most often (tallest bar), then turn it into a measurable characteristic.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Tallest bar: “Slow speed”."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Speed is 44.6% of all comments; with outages it reaches 73.0%."},{title:"Decide",body:"Turn the top VOC theme into a measurable CTQ — delivered speed versus plan speed → answer **B**."}],final:"Slow speed = 44.6% of VOC → speed CTQ first → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Price is third, at 11.9% of comments — it is not the strongest customer voice here.","","App issues are only 7.5% of comments.","Complaint handling is not one of the coded themes; this reflects an internal (business) view, not what customers said."],figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Coded Customer Comments",categories:[{name:"Slow speed",count:344},{name:"Outages",count:219},{name:"Price",count:92},{name:"App issues",count:58},{name:"Contract",count:33},{name:"Other",count:25}],yLabel:"Comments"},bookRef:"Book pp. 112, 586"},{id:"sv-1-419",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"The first-level Pareto showed that **Billing errors** were the largest complaint category. The team then charts the billing errors only, by sub-cause. Which statement about this chart is TRUE?",options:["It is a second-level Pareto; “Wrong plan” and “Late sync” together cover 76.4% of billing errors and should be the focus","It is a first-level Pareto, so the team should go back and choose a different category","“Wrong plan” covers 43.3% of all customer complaints","Because the bars are steep, the team can skip data collection in Measure"],answer:0,hint:"A Pareto built on the sub-causes of one bar is a lower-level Pareto; its percentages are shares of that bar only.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Title and total: the chart contains billing errors only."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Two sub-causes reach 76.4% of the billing errors."},{title:"Decide",body:"This drill-down narrows the scope to Wrong plan and Late sync → answer **A**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Percentages on a drill-down chart are shares of the parent category, not of the whole."}],final:"Second-level Pareto, top two = 76.4% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Charting the sub-causes of one bar is the definition of a second-level (drill-down) Pareto.","43.3% is its share of **billing errors** only, not of all complaints.","A Pareto narrows the focus; it does not replace a baseline and measurement-system check in Measure."],trap:"On a drill-down Pareto, the 100% is the parent bar, not the whole data set.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Billing Errors by Sub-cause",categories:[{name:"Wrong plan",count:158},{name:"Late sync",count:121},{name:"Manual adj",count:37},{name:"Rate table",count:26},{name:"FX rate",count:14},{name:"Other",count:9}],yLabel:"Billing errors"},bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-420",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:3,stem:"A shop-operations Green Belt charts defects found on new-line application forms. The Champion asks: “What is the **smallest** number of defect types we must fix to remove **at least 80%** of the defects?”",options:["2 defect types","3 defect types","4 defect types","5 defect types"],answer:2,hint:"Find the first bar whose Cum % is 80 or more; count the bars up to and including it.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Cum % row: 34.4, 60.0, 76.4, 87.3, 93.6, 97.8, 100.0."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Bar 3 gives 76.4% (< 80%); bar 4 gives 87.3% (≥ 80%)."},{title:"Decide",body:"The smallest number is **4** → answer **C**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“About 80%” is not “at least 80%” — read the exact wording."}],final:"Cum % passes 80 at bar 4 (87.3%) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Two bars reach only 60.0%.","Three bars give 76.4% — close to 80% but still **below** it.","","Five bars (93.6%) work, but four already pass 80%, so five is not the smallest number."],trap:"Exact wording matters: “at least 80%” rules out a bar that gives 78.8%.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Application Form Defects",categories:[{name:"Address",count:203},{name:"ID scan",count:151},{name:"Signature",count:97},{name:"Plan code",count:64},{name:"Payment",count:37},{name:"Email",count:25},{name:"Other",count:13}],yLabel:"Defects"},bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-421",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"graph",difficulty:3,stem:`An audit checks **200** customer contracts and records every defect found on each one. The Pareto chart of the defects is shown (total = 270).

Which statement is TRUE?`,options:["270 of the 200 contracts were defective","35.6% of the contracts were missing an ID","Because the total is larger than 200, the data must contain errors and the chart should be discarded","The bars count defects, so one contract can appear in more than one bar; the chart cannot show how many contracts were defective"],answer:3,hint:"Pareto bars count occurrences. Defects are counted per occurrence; a defective unit is counted once however many defects it has.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Compare the total (270) with the number of contracts checked (200)."},{title:"What it shows",body:"270 > 200, so some contracts carry more than one defect — the chart counts **defects**, not defective units."},{title:"Decide",body:"The number of defective contracts (and FTY) cannot be read from this chart → answer **D**."}],final:"Pareto counts defects, not defectives → answer **D**",whyWrong:["270 is the number of **defects**; a contract with three defects is counted three times.","35.6% is the share of all **defects**, not of contracts (96 of 200 contracts would be 48%).","A total above the number of units is normal when units can carry several defects.",""],trap:"Defect ≠ defective: DPU uses defects, FTY uses defective-free units.",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Contract Defects",categories:[{name:"Missing ID",count:96},{name:"No sign",count:74},{name:"Bad addr",count:51},{name:"Plan code",count:33},{name:"Other",count:16}],yLabel:"Defects"},bookRef:"Book pp. 585, 588"},{id:"sv-1-422",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:3,stem:"The draft goal statement says: “Reduce late device deliveries by **50%** by fixing stock-outs.” The Pareto chart of late-delivery reasons is shown. Which statement about the goal is correct?",options:["The goal is realistic because stock-out is the tallest bar","Even removing every stock-out would cut late deliveries by only 46.2%, so the goal or the scope must change","The goal is realistic because stock-out and courier together give 73.8%","The goal cannot be judged until the Analyze phase"],answer:1,hint:"The most a project can gain from one category is that category’s share of the total — compare it with the goal.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Percent under the Stock-out bar: 46.2%."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Even a perfect fix of stock-outs removes at most 46.2% of late deliveries."},{title:"Decide",body:"A 50% goal needs a wider scope (for example stock-out **and** courier, 73.8%) or a lower target → answer **B**."}],final:"Max gain from stock-outs = 46.2% < 50% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Tallest does not mean big enough: stock-outs are 46.2% of late deliveries, below the 50% target.","","The goal names stock-outs only; the courier share is outside the stated scope.","The Pareto already shows the most the stated scope can deliver."],trap:"Check that the goal is achievable within the scope (the A and R of SMART).",figure:{kind:"pareto",title:"Pareto Chart of Late Delivery Reason",categories:[{name:"Stock-out",count:215},{name:"Courier",count:128},{name:"Address",count:61},{name:"Payment",count:34},{name:"Picking",count:22},{name:"Other",count:5}],yLabel:"Late deliveries"},bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 588"},{id:"sv-1-423",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:"To set the baseline for the project charter, a Green Belt plots the **average handle time (seconds)** for 30 consecutive working days. Which statement about the run chart is TRUE?",options:["The data vary randomly around a median of about 312 s with no trend or shift, so the median can serve as the baseline","There is a clear upward trend, so the baseline will be out of date before Measure ends","The points cluster in long runs above and below the median, signalling a shift","The highest point must be removed before the baseline is calculated"],answer:0,hint:"Read the four approximate P-values. Any P < 0.05 signals clustering, mixtures, trends or oscillation; otherwise the pattern is random.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The stats box: P clustering, P mixtures, P trends, P oscillation."},{title:"What it shows",body:"All four P-values are above 0.05 (0.500, 0.500, 0.559, 0.441): only random variation."},{title:"Decide",body:"A stable process → the median (~312 s) is a fair baseline → answer **A**."}],final:"All run-chart P-values > 0.05 → stable baseline → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Runs up/down = 20 with P trends = 0.559 (> 0.05): no trend is signalled.","P clustering = 0.500 (> 0.05) — the runs about the median look random.","No point stands out; removing data without a known special cause biases the baseline."],figure:{kind:"run",title:"Run Chart of Average Handle Time",data:[307.5,305.2,335.5,339.6,314.2,294.4,314.8,312.6,299.7,333.4,325.9,292.3,324.4,292,305.9,299.6,301.6,312,305.4,307.6,330.4,307.8,317.9,290.5,314.7,321.8,313.7,303.8,326.7,332.3],yLabel:"AHT (s)",stats:[["Median","312.30"],["Runs about median","16"],["Expected runs","16.0"],["P clustering","0.500"],["P mixtures","0.500"],["Runs up/down","20"],["P trends","0.559"],["P oscillation","0.441"]]},bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-424",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt plots **daily % of orders with activation errors** for the last 30 days to quote a baseline in the problem statement. Which conclusion is correct?",options:["The process is stable, so the 30-day median is a valid baseline","The pattern shows oscillation, so the data were probably collected from two alternating teams","The data cluster below the median early and above it later — a shift; find out what changed around day 15 before choosing the baseline period","The pattern shows a gradual trend, so the baseline should be extrapolated to next month"],answer:2,hint:"Few runs about the median (P clustering < 0.05) = clusters or a shift. Look for where the points move from one side of the median to the other.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Stats box: Runs about median = 4 vs expected 16.0; P clustering = 0.000."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Days 1–14 sit mostly below the median, days 15–30 mostly above — a step change in the level."},{title:"Decide",body:"Identify the cause of the shift (a release? a new team?) and base the problem statement on the current level → answer **C**."}],final:"P clustering = 0.000 → shift → answer **C**",whyWrong:["P clustering = 0.000 (< 0.05): the process is not stable over the 30 days.","P oscillation = 0.441 (> 0.05); the points do not zig-zag, they sit in two long runs.","","P trends = 0.559 (> 0.05). The change is a step, not a steady slope."],trap:"Clustering = too FEW runs; mixtures = too MANY runs.",figure:{kind:"run",title:"Run Chart of Daily Activation Error %",data:[4.64,4.28,4.67,3.7,4.1,4.22,4.29,4.3,3.58,4.58,4,4.4,4.33,4.36,5.63,6.07,5.29,5.43,5.31,6.64,6.02,5.42,4.9,5.62,5.98,5.55,5,5.58,5.19,5.48],yLabel:"Activation errors (%)",stats:[["Median","4.95"],["Runs about median","4"],["Expected runs","16.0"],["P clustering","0.000"],["P mixtures","1.000"],["Runs up/down","20"],["P trends","0.559"],["P oscillation","0.441"]]},bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-425",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"The business case for a project needs evidence of “why now”. The run chart shows **monthly first-contact resolution (FCR %)** for the last 24 months. Which statement best supports the business case?",options:["FCR varies randomly around its median, so there is no urgency","FCR oscillates month to month, so the data come from two different systems","The last month is the lowest, so a single special cause explains the problem","FCR has fallen steadily from about 82% to about 72%; without action the decline is likely to continue"],answer:3,hint:"A long slide in one direction (P trends or P clustering < 0.05) is strong “why now” evidence: the problem is getting worse.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The overall direction of the points and the P-values in the box."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Early months sit above the median and later months below it; P clustering = 0.000, P trends = 0.009."},{title:"Decide",body:"A worsening trend is the strongest “why now” argument for the charter → answer **D**."}],final:"Downward trend → urgency for the business case → answer **D**",whyWrong:["P clustering = 0.000 and P trends = 0.009: the pattern is not random.","P oscillation = 0.991 — there is no zig-zag pattern.","The decline runs across the whole period, not just one month.",""],figure:{kind:"run",title:"Run Chart of Monthly FCR %",data:[83.3,81.6,81.1,80.5,80.7,79.9,78.4,79.2,78.5,78.6,76.1,73.9,75.6,76.2,75.2,74.3,74,75.7,73.6,72.3,72.1,71.7,71.5,71.4],yLabel:"FCR (%)",stats:[["Median","75.90"],["Runs about median","4"],["Expected runs","13.0"],["P clustering","0.000"],["P mixtures","1.000"],["Runs up/down","11"],["P trends","0.009"],["P oscillation","0.991"]]},bookRef:"Book pp. 230, 589"},{id:"sv-1-426",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt plots **monthly roaming bill-shock complaints** for 24 months (month 1 = January). The Champion suggests using **March–May** of this year as the baseline “because those months are recent”. Based on the run chart, what is the best response?",options:["Agree — the most recent three months are always the best baseline","Use only the two December peaks, because they show the worst case","The data follow a yearly cycle that peaks around December; the baseline should cover at least one full 12-month cycle","The data are random, so any three months give the same baseline"],answer:2,hint:"Look for a repeating pattern. If the process has a cycle, the baseline period must cover the full cycle.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"High values around each December–January (months 1, 12–13 and 24) and lows in mid-year (months 6 and 18); P clustering = 0.000."},{title:"What it shows",body:"A seasonal (yearly) cycle: complaints rise towards the year-end travel season and fall mid-year."},{title:"Decide",body:"Use at least 12 consecutive months so the baseline is representative → answer **C**."}],final:"Seasonal cycle → baseline over a full year → answer **C**",whyWrong:["March–May sit in the low part of the yearly cycle; a baseline from them would understate the problem.","Peaks alone overstate the problem; the baseline must represent the whole cycle.","","P clustering = 0.000 (< 0.05) and the rise-and-fall pattern repeats — the months are not interchangeable."],trap:"A baseline must be representative of the process, not just recent.",figure:{kind:"run",title:"Run Chart of Monthly Bill-Shock Complaints",data:[16458,15042,13887,13034,11837,11341,11933,13072,14764,15673,15612,16407,15975,15749,14159,12874,11681,10915,11095,11716,14309,14757,16264,16940],yLabel:"Complaints",stats:[["Median","14234.00"],["Runs about median","5"],["Expected runs","13.0"],["P clustering","0.000"],["P mixtures","1.000"],["Runs up/down","6"],["P trends","0.000"],["P oscillation","1.000"]]},bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 589"},{id:"sv-1-427",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"The CTQ for business-line repairs is “restore service within **48 hours**”. The histogram shows 100 recent repair times. Which statement is TRUE?",options:["The average repair time is above the USL, so most repairs fail the CTQ","The average repair meets the CTQ, but about 5% of repairs exceed 48 h because of the spread","No repair exceeds the USL, so the CTQ is fully met","The CTQ should be set at the average (about 40 h) so that the chart looks better"],answer:1,hint:"Compare the mean with the spec line, then check how much of the distribution lies beyond it — customers feel the tail, not the average.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The red USL line at 48 h and the “% > USL” row."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Mean = 40.9 h (inside spec) but StDev = 4.59 h, so 5.0% of repairs fall above 48 h."},{title:"Decide",body:"The average looks fine; the defects come from variation → answer **B**."}],final:"Mean 40.9 h < USL but 5.0% above → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The mean is 40.9 h — below the 48 h USL.","","The stats box shows 5.0% of repairs above the USL; bars cross the red line.","A CTQ comes from the customer requirement, not from current performance."],trap:"Customers feel the variation, not the average.",figure:{kind:"histogram",title:"Histogram of Repair Time (h)",data:[38.2,32.5,43.5,46.1,34,41.9,47.6,31.7,36.9,37.5,43.6,37.5,40.2,44,37.3,32.9,40.5,44,46,40,35.5,36.4,46.9,36.7,41.9,38,50.9,40.2,45.7,51.4,41.5,43.2,44.1,44,34,37.7,45.7,38.7,38.2,31.6,43.2,44.8,40.3,42.3,39.8,35.2,41.8,47,40.2,42.2,45,47.3,42.4,41,43,38.7,46.9,36.4,28.9,44,38.2,45.8,39.1,40.6,39.6,38.6,42.1,48.7,44.1,37.8,41.7,40.8,40.2,44.8,35.8,36.4,37.8,43.7,42.5,38.8,44.7,36.4,48.3,44.4,31.7,41.1,47.5,38,42.3,33.5,36,37.5,35.5,38.4,44.5,47.9,42.2,38.6,42.6,48.3],usl:48,normalCurve:!0,xLabel:"Repair time (h)",stats:[["N","100"],["Mean","40.85"],["StDev","4.59"],["USL","48"],["% > USL","5.0"]]},bookRef:"Book pp. 162, 586"},{id:"sv-1-428",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"An operations team pools **fibre installation lead times (days)** from its two regional depots into one histogram to describe current performance against the CTQ. What is the most likely explanation of the shape?",options:["Two different processes (for example the two depots) are mixed; the data should be stratified before a baseline is quoted","The data are right-skewed because lead times cannot be negative","The process is normal; the gap in the middle is just sampling noise","The measurement system rounds to whole days"],answer:0,hint:"Two humps (bimodal) usually mean two populations mixed in one data set.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The overall shape: two peaks, around 3 and 6 days."},{title:"What it shows",body:"A bimodal distribution; the single mean (4.4 days) falls in the valley where few installs actually occur."},{title:"Decide",body:"Split the data by depot (or another factor) and baseline each one → answer **A**."}],final:"Bimodal → mixed populations → stratify → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A skewed shape has one peak and a long tail; this histogram has two separate peaks.","With 100 points, a deep valley between two clear peaks is not random noise.","Rounding causes gaps between every bar, not two separate humps."],trap:"The mean of a bimodal data set describes almost no real installation.",figure:{kind:"histogram",title:"Histogram of Installation Lead Time",data:[2.3,2.8,2.2,2.1,3,2.9,2.4,2.6,2.8,2.9,2.4,2.8,2.2,3.4,2.5,3,3.4,2.8,3.4,2.4,3,2.3,2.4,2.8,3.4,1.9,2.8,2.6,3.1,2.5,2.7,3.3,2.6,2.6,3.7,2.7,2.9,2.7,2.7,2.4,2.1,2.4,3,2.5,3.5,3.2,3,2.7,2.9,3.2,6.6,6.5,6.4,5.3,5.6,5.7,6.1,6.8,6.5,5.7,6.5,6.8,5.9,6.7,6.3,5.7,5.9,5.8,6,5.4,6.3,5.5,5.9,6.1,6.1,4.5,6.4,5.8,7.4,6.1,6.3,6.2,5.5,5.4,6.2,6,5.5,5.6,5.6,6.3,6,6.2,6.5,6,6.4,5.5,6.8,6.4,6.1,5.9],bins:14,xLabel:"Lead time (days)",stats:[["N","100"],["Mean","4.41"],["StDev","1.72"]]},bookRef:"Book p. 162"},{id:"sv-1-429",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:3,stem:"Technicians are expected to arrive within **±30 minutes** of the booked slot (0 = on time, positive = late). The histogram shows 100 recent visits. Which project goal does the graph support?",options:["Reduce the spread of arrival times, because the distribution is too wide for the window","Widen the window to ±45 minutes so that all visits comply","No project is needed because Pp is above 1.33","Re-centre arrival times on the booked slot; the spread already fits well inside the window"],answer:3,hint:"Compare Pp (spread only) with Ppk (spread and centring). A big gap between them means the process is off-centre.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Mean = 18.0 min against a target of 0; Pp = 1.57 vs Ppk = 0.63."},{title:"What it shows",body:"The distribution is narrow enough for the window but sits towards the late (USL) side."},{title:"Decide",body:"The goal should move the mean to 0 (centring), not cut variation → answer **D**."}],final:"Pp 1.57 vs Ppk 0.63 → off-centre → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Pp = 1.57: the spread would fit the ±30 min window comfortably if it were centred.","Changing the customer requirement to fit the process is not an improvement goal.","Pp ignores centring; Ppk = 0.63 shows the process runs close to the late limit.",""],trap:"Pp high + Ppk low = a centring problem, not a spread problem.",figure:{kind:"histogram",title:"Histogram of Arrival vs Booked Slot",data:[20,14,19,11,25,20,15,31,26,24,11,18,20,12,28,17,24,16,19,12,16,21,7,14,17,17,3,15,13,10,16,22,9,14,12,14,24,18,22,23,13,4,26,15,21,11,28,16,13,15,16,16,15,23,6,24,31,22,8,11,19,10,23,10,14,21,23,19,28,29,27,10,12,18,22,20,26,18,12,31,23,25,22,24,26,22,14,21,27,15,6,19,16,19,27,17,14,19,7,21],lsl:-30,usl:30,target:0,normalCurve:!0,xLabel:"Minutes late (+) / early (−)",stats:[["N","100"],["Mean","17.99"],["StDev","6.38"],["Pp","1.57"],["Ppk","0.63"],["% out of spec","3.0"]]},bookRef:"Book pp. 162, 231"},{id:"sv-1-430",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:"The CTQ for the contact centre is “answer calls within **60 seconds**”. The histogram shows 100 sampled waiting times. Which statement about the shape is TRUE?",options:["The data are right-skewed: most calls are answered quickly, but a long tail of slow answers crosses the USL","The data are left-skewed, with a tail towards short waits","The data are symmetric, so the mean and median are about equal","The data are bimodal, showing two separate call queues"],answer:0,hint:"Skew is named after the side of the long tail. In right-skewed data the mean sits above the median.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The bars pile up on the left and stretch out to the right; compare Mean and Median."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Long right tail; Mean (29.8) > Median (26.0); 10% of calls exceed 60 s."},{title:"Decide",body:"Right-skewed with a tail beyond the USL → answer **A**."}],final:"Long right tail, mean > median → right-skewed → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The long tail points to the right (long waits), so the data are right-skewed.","Mean 29.8 s vs median 26.0 s — the mean is pulled up by the right tail.","There is one peak, with a tail — not two humps."],figure:{kind:"histogram",title:"Histogram of Call Waiting Time (s)",data:[17,9,80,30,32,27,36,29,29,31,31,62,8,65,50,37,64,22,26,36,47,9,66,49,47,41,12,15,48,29,19,18,31,12,39,15,20,36,19,51,15,35,34,29,41,52,10,20,16,21,68,41,12,25,19,32,7,19,23,11,38,32,12,26,8,8,20,11,15,26,30,12,10,23,7,31,22,40,22,24,16,38,32,48,22,13,78,14,74,23,66,18,78,22,27,20,15,26,22,39],usl:60,xLabel:"Waiting time (s)",stats:[["N","100"],["Mean","29.82"],["Median","26.00"],["StDev","17.71"],["% > USL","10.0"]]},bookRef:"Book pp. 162, 586"},{id:"sv-1-431",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:"A Green Belt compares **fault-repair time (hours)** across three regions against the 6-hour target (dashed line). Which statement about the boxplot is TRUE?",options:["All three regions have similar medians, so region is not worth stratifying","North has the widest spread of repair times","Interior has the highest median and the widest spread; most of its repairs miss the 6-hour target","South’s box lies mostly above the target line"],answer:2,hint:"Medians are the lines inside the boxes; spread is the box height (IQR) and whisker length. Compare both with the reference line.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Median lines and box heights against the dashed 6-hour line."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Interior: median ≈ 8.1 h, box well above 6 h, tallest box. North and South: medians ≈ 5.1–5.6 h, below the line."},{title:"Decide",body:"Interior is both slower and more variable → answer **C**."}],final:"Interior: highest median, widest box, above target → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Interior’s median (~8.1 h) is far above North (~5.1 h) and South (~5.6 h).","North has the narrowest box (IQR ≈ 0.8 h); Interior’s box is the widest (≈ 1.4 h).","","Most of South’s box sits below the 6-hour line; only the top edge of the box and the upper whisker cross it."],figure:{kind:"boxplot",title:"Boxplot of Repair Time by Region",groups:[{name:"North",data:[5.4,5.1,6.2,6.4,4,5.4,4.9,5.1,4.1,5.6,4.8,5,4.2,5.7,5,5.2,5.6,6.4,5.1,5,5,6.1,5.8,4,6.2]},{name:"South",data:[5.8,5.4,5.6,5.3,4.1,4.8,6.3,5.6,6.6,6.1,5,5.7,5.6,4.8,5.6,6.4,6.2,5.7,5,6.3,4.3,5.7,6.1,7.8,4.3]},{name:"Interior",data:[8.9,7.5,8.1,6.7,9.7,8.3,8,7.8,7,8.2,7.3,8.7,8.2,8.4,9.1,7.6,7.5,8.3,6.6,9,8.4,6.8,6.7,6.9,9.3]}],yLabel:"Repair time (h)",xLabel:"Region",refLine:6},bookRef:"Book p. 163"},{id:"sv-1-432",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"In Define, a Green Belt plots **average handle time (s)** — the project Y — by agent tenure band. What is the most appropriate conclusion?",options:["Tenure has been proven to cause long handle times, so the solution is to hire only experienced agents","Tenure looks like a candidate X that may drive handle time; record it and verify the relationship in Analyze","Handle time is an input (X) and tenure is the output (Y)","The boxes overlap a little, so tenure has no effect on handle time"],answer:1,hint:"Y = f(X): the Y is the outcome you measure; the X’s are inputs that may drive it. A graph suggests X’s; Analyze confirms them.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The median line in each tenure band."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Median handle time falls as tenure rises: a visible link between an input and the Y."},{title:"Decide",body:"Treat tenure as a **candidate** X and test it in Analyze → answer **B**."}],final:"Graph suggests a candidate X, not proof → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A boxplot shows an association; causation must be confirmed with data in Analyze before choosing solutions.","","Handle time is the project Y; tenure is a possible input X — the roles are reversed.","The medians fall clearly from about 408 s to about 312 s; small overlap does not mean no effect."],trap:"Correlation in a graph is not causation.",figure:{kind:"boxplot",title:"Boxplot of AHT by Agent Tenure",groups:[{name:"< 6 mo",data:[362,399,402,361,506,412,435,478,414,325,452,405,367,397,450,398,411,427,398,423]},{name:"6–24 mo",data:[374,363,358,316,362,361,388,351,356,355,348,335,276,354,326,313,379,350,307,344]},{name:"> 24 mo",data:[311,255,338,306,347,363,286,301,290,337,310,340,319,327,299,266,322,317,313,263]}],yLabel:"AHT (s)",xLabel:"Tenure band"},bookRef:"Book pp. 113–114, 163"},{id:"sv-1-433",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:3,stem:"The CTQ for SIM delivery is “delivered within **30 hours**” (dashed line). The boxplot compares day-shift and night-shift dispatches. Which statement is NOT supported by the boxplot?",options:["The night shift has a lower median delivery time than the day shift","The night shift shows more variation than the day shift","Some night-shift deliveries exceed the 30-hour CTQ","All day-shift deliveries meet the 30-hour CTQ"],answer:0,hint:"Check each statement against the median lines, box heights, whiskers and the reference line; pick the one the picture contradicts.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Median lines: day ≈ 21.5 h, night ≈ 29.0 h; the 30-hour dashed line."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Night has the higher median and the taller box, and its upper part crosses 30 h; the day whisker stays below 30 h."},{title:"Decide",body:"The claim that night has a **lower** median is contradicted → answer **A**."}],final:"Night median is higher, not lower → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Supported — the night box (IQR ≈ 13.0 h) is much taller than the day box (≈ 4.2 h).","Supported — the night box’s upper quartile (~34.0 h) is above the 30-hour line.","Supported — the day upper whisker ends at 26 h and there are no outliers above it."],trap:"In NOT questions, three options are true — find the one the graph contradicts.",figure:{kind:"boxplot",title:"Boxplot of SIM Delivery Time by Shift",groups:[{name:"Day",data:[20,23,21,24,22,26,19,21,26,20,21,26,22,19,23,21,26,26,25,20,21,25,22,18,20,19,20,19,23,24]},{name:"Night",data:[14,34,23,16,34,36,35,17,23,36,31,31,28,18,24,34,30,22,42,34,19,21,21,30,29,37,26,34,20,29]}],yLabel:"Delivery time (h)",xLabel:"Dispatch shift",refLine:30},bookRef:"Book pp. 163, 586"},{id:"sv-1-434",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"The charter goal is **95%** of tickets resolved within SLA. The I-MR chart shows the last 25 days of baseline data (no tests failed). What does the chart tell the team?",options:["The process is out of control, so the team must remove special causes first","Some days already reach 95%, so better day-to-day supervision will meet the goal","The MR chart is out of control, so the I chart cannot be read","The process is stable around 90.9%; even its UCL (93.7%) is below 95%, so the goal needs a change to the process itself"],answer:3,hint:"Read the MR chart first, then the I chart. Stable but off-target means a common-cause problem: the system must change.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"MR chart: no flags; I chart: no flags; then the UCL against the 95% goal."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Stable, predictable process: X̄ = 90.9%, UCL = 93.7%. The goal lies above the whole band of common-cause variation."},{title:"Decide",body:"A stable process that cannot reach the goal needs a fundamental (system) change — a DMAIC project → answer **D**."}],final:"Stable, UCL 93.7% < 95% goal → answer **D**",whyWrong:["No point is flagged on either chart — only common-cause variation is present.","No day reaches 95%; the highest point is 92.6%. Common-cause variation cannot be “supervised away”.","The MR chart has no flagged points, so the I chart limits are valid.",""],trap:"In control does not mean capable or on target.",figure:{kind:"control",title:"I-MR Chart of Daily SLA Met %",xLabel:"Day",panels:[{label:"Individual Value",points:[90.2,91.1,92.6,92.2,90.4,91.4,90.9,90.6,91.4,90.5,91.4,89.2,90.6,89.3,91.5,91.9,91.1,91.5,90.9,90.6,89.4,91.5,91.6,89.9,91.3],cl:90.92,ucl:93.702,lcl:88.138,clLabel:"X̄=90.920"},{label:"Moving Range",points:[null,.9,1.5,.4,1.8,1,.5,.3,.8,.9,.9,2.2,1.4,1.3,2.2,.4,.8,.4,.6,.3,1.2,2.1,.1,1.7,1.4],cl:1.046,ucl:3.417,lcl:0,clLabel:"MR̄=1.046"}]},bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 527"},{id:"sv-1-435",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:3,stem:"A Green Belt prepares the baseline for the problem statement. Day 17 on the I-MR chart is the day of a region-wide power outage. How should the team treat day 17?",options:["Average it in with the other days, because all data must be used","Delete it silently so that the chart looks stable","Investigate and document it as a special cause, then quote the baseline from the stable days and state the exclusion","Recalculate the limits so that day 17 falls inside them"],answer:2,hint:"Test 1 = one point beyond 3σ. Find the reason for a special cause; exclude it from the baseline only when the reason is known — and record it.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Read the MR chart first: the jump into and out of day 17 shows as flagged ranges. On the I chart day 17 is above UCL = 56.4 (Test 1)."},{title:"What it shows",body:"One assignable special cause (the outage); all other days vary randomly around the centre line."},{title:"Decide",body:"Document the outage, exclude it with justification, and base the problem statement on the routine process → answer **C**."}],final:"Known special cause → investigate, document, exclude → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Day 17 fails Test 1 — it is a special cause, not part of the routine process the project will improve.","Removing data without investigation and documentation hides information and is not acceptable.","","Limits come from the data; moving them to hide a signal defeats the purpose of the chart."],trap:"Never delete a point without a documented, assignable cause.",figure:{kind:"control",title:"I-MR Chart of Daily Ticket Backlog (hundreds)",xLabel:"Day",panels:[{label:"Individual Value",points:[45.8,47.3,48.2,41.8,44.7,42.2,49.7,46.5,43.9,47.5,46.8,47.3,46.7,44.7,43,48.4,61.5,45.6,46.3,44.4,46.3,43.6,47.3,43.7,48.2],cl:46.456,ucl:56.431,lcl:36.481,clLabel:"X̄=46.456",flags:[{i:16,test:"1"}]},{label:"Moving Range",points:[null,1.5,.9,6.4,2.9,2.5,7.5,3.2,2.6,3.6,.7,.5,.6,2,1.7,5.4,13.1,15.9,.7,1.9,1.9,2.7,3.7,3.6,4.5],cl:3.75,ucl:12.251,lcl:0,clLabel:"MR̄=3.750",flags:[{i:16,test:"1"},{i:17,test:"1"}]}]},bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 527"},{id:"sv-1-436",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:"After a server migration on day 13, the team tracks **average app login time (seconds)** on an I-MR chart. Which day is the first to signal a special cause, and by which test?",options:["Day 13, Test 1 (one point beyond 3σ)","Day 29, Test 1 (one point beyond 3σ)","Day 30, Test 2 (nine points in a row on the same side of the centre line)","Day 29, Test 2 (nine points in a row on the same side of the centre line)"],answer:3,hint:"Flagged points are red squares labelled with the test number. The first flag marks the point that completes the pattern.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"MR chart first: no flags. Then the I chart: find the leftmost red square and its label."},{title:"What it shows",body:"After day 13 most points fall below X̄ = 11.60, but days 15, 16 and 20 are above it, so the run restarts. From day 21 every point is below the centre line; the ninth in a row (day 29) is flagged “2”."},{title:"Decide",body:"Day 29, Test 2 → answer **D**."}],final:"First flag = day 29, Test 2 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Day 13 is not beyond either limit and carries no flag; the migration day is not itself a signal.","The flag label on day 29 reads “2”: it completes a run, it is not beyond a limit.","Later days are also flagged, but day 29 is the **first** point that completes the run.",""],trap:"Test 2 marks the 9th point of the run, not the first point after the change.",figure:{kind:"control",title:"I-MR Chart of Avg Login Time (s)",xLabel:"Day",panels:[{label:"Individual Value",points:[11.5,12.66,12.89,12,12.56,12.1,12.27,12.28,10.92,11.84,13.28,12.16,10.86,11.78,12.16,12.44,11.36,10.27,11.04,12.52,10.45,10.63,11.25,11.33,10.44,10.44,11.18,11.48,10.9,10.9],cl:11.596,ucl:13.53,lcl:9.663,clLabel:"X̄=11.596",flags:[{i:28,test:"2"},{i:29,test:"2"}]},{label:"Moving Range",points:[null,1.16,.23,.89,.56,.46,.17,.01,1.36,.92,1.44,1.12,1.3,.92,.38,.28,1.08,1.09,.77,1.48,2.07,.18,.62,.08,.89,0,.74,.3,.58,0],cl:.727,ucl:2.375,lcl:0,clLabel:"MR̄=0.727"}]},bookRef:"Book p. 527"},{id:"sv-1-437",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:"Both customer segments have the same CTQ: “first response within **4 hours**”. The dotplot compares first-response times for enterprise and consumer tickets. Which statement is TRUE?",options:["The two segments have similar centres, but enterprise tickets are far more spread out, with several above 4 hours","Consumer tickets show the wider spread","Enterprise tickets have a much higher average, which is why they miss the CTQ","Neither segment has any ticket above 4 hours"],answer:0,hint:"In a dotplot, compare where each group is centred and how far its dots stretch.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The centre and the width of each row of dots; mentally mark 4 h on the axis."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Centres are similar (≈ 3.5 h vs 3.2 h); enterprise dots spread from about 1.1 to 5.5 h, with 10 above 4 h."},{title:"Decide",body:"The enterprise problem is variation → answer **A**."}],final:"Same centre, enterprise much wider → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The consumer dots are tightly bunched; the enterprise dots stretch across the axis.","The centres are close (≈ 3.5 h vs 3.2 h); the misses come from the spread, not from a higher centre.","10 enterprise tickets lie above 4 h."],figure:{kind:"dotplot",title:"Dotplot of First Response Time by Segment",groups:[{name:"Enterprise",data:[4.3,2.3,5.5,3.2,2.2,2.9,4.7,4.8,2.1,3.7,4,5.4,2.3,1.1,3.1,3.8,4.2,4.9,3.1,3,3.4,4.6,1.7,3.3,2.3,4.4,4.8,2.5]},{name:"Consumer",data:[3.5,2.9,3.5,3.5,2.3,2.9,3.4,3.5,3.2,3.4,3,3.6,3.1,3.7,3.6,2.8,2.9,3.7,2.4,2.9,2.8,3.6,3.6,3.2,2.9,2.9,3.3,3]}],xLabel:"First response time (h)"},bookRef:"Book pp. 163, 586"},{id:"sv-1-438",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"graph",difficulty:2,stem:"The dotplot shows the **end-to-end lead time (days)** of 45 postpaid orders. Most orders finish in under 2 days, but a separate cluster takes about 5 days. What is the most likely explanation of that cluster?",options:["The cluster is normal common-cause variation of a single process","Those orders went through a rework loop (rejected and re-submitted) — part of the hidden factory that adds cost of poor quality","The cluster shows the customers’ requested delivery dates","The cluster proves the order system is too slow and must be replaced"],answer:1,hint:"A separate cluster means some units follow a different path. In service processes the usual hidden path is rework.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The gap between the main cluster (≈ 1–2 days) and the small cluster (≈ 5 days)."},{title:"What it shows",body:"9 of 45 orders follow a much longer path — typical of orders that are rejected and re-processed."},{title:"Decide",body:"Rework is hidden-factory COPQ (internal failure) → answer **B**."}],final:"Separate slow cluster → rework / hidden factory → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Common-cause variation gives one continuous spread, not a separate group with a gap of about 3 days.","","Requested dates would not form a tight extra cluster at about 5 days separate from the main group.","The graph shows a separate path; it does not show the cause or justify a solution yet."],figure:{kind:"dotplot",title:"Dotplot of Order Lead Time",groups:[{name:"Orders",data:[1.7,1.8,1.3,1.5,1.8,1.1,1,1.8,1.4,1.3,1.3,1.1,1.4,1.3,1.7,1.3,1.2,1.5,1.5,1.5,2,1.2,1.8,.9,1.6,1.1,1.7,1.6,1.1,1.6,1.2,1.7,1.4,1.8,1.6,1.8,4.4,5.3,4.9,4.9,4.1,4.6,5.8,6,5.2]}],xLabel:"Lead time (days)"},bookRef:"Book pp. 163, 585"},{id:"sv-1-439",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"graph",difficulty:1,stem:"Finance compares the monthly **rework cost (OMR)** of the order-fallout process last year (baseline) with this year, when the project solution went live. From which month did the savings begin to appear?",options:["January","June","April","September"],answer:1,hint:"Benefits show as a gap between the baseline line and the project-year line; find the first month where the lines separate.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"The two lines month by month."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Until May the lines overlap; from June this year’s cost drops (≈ 23800 vs 30500 OMR) and the gap widens."},{title:"Decide",body:"Savings start in June → answer **B**."}],final:"Lines separate in June → answer **B**",whyWrong:["January–May track last year’s line closely; there is no gap yet.","","April is still on the baseline line; the gap opens one or two months later.","By September the savings are already large; the drop started in June."],figure:{kind:"lines",title:"Monthly Rework Cost: Baseline vs Project Year",xLabels:["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"],series:[{name:"Last year",values:[33300,29900,32100,32200,31300,30500,31300,30600,3e4,30700,31800,30300]},{name:"This year",values:[33300,30100,31600,31600,31400,23800,20700,18400,17400,17500,17800,17300]}],yLabel:"Cost (OMR)",xLabel:"Month"},bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 585"},{id:"sv-1-440",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"graph",difficulty:3,stem:"The charter goal is “repeat calls **≤ 10%** of calls”. The solution went live in May. The Sponsor wants to close the project and book the full annual benefit now. Based on the graph, what is the best response?",options:["Close now — the trend shows the goal has been reached","Close now and book the benefit from January, because the goal was set in January","Not yet — the goal is met only in the latest month; keep tracking until the gain is sustained before booking the full benefit","Abandon the project — repeat calls are still above 10% in most months"],answer:2,hint:"Benefits are booked from verified, sustained results — compare the actual line with the goal line over several months.",steps:[{title:"Where to look",body:"Where the Actual line crosses the Goal line."},{title:"What it shows",body:"Repeat calls fall steadily after May but drop below 10% only in December."},{title:"Decide",body:"Keep measuring (usually with Finance) until the gain holds, then confirm the benefit → answer **C**."}],final:"Goal met in one month only → keep tracking → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Only December is at or below 10%; one month does not prove a sustained gain.","Benefits can only be counted from when the results actually improved, and they must be verified.","","The clear downward trend since May shows the solution is working; it simply needs more time to confirm."],trap:"Projected benefits ≠ realised benefits.",figure:{kind:"lines",title:"Repeat Calls % vs Charter Goal",xLabels:["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"],series:[{name:"Actual",values:[14.8,15.1,14.6,15.3,13.9,12.4,11.6,11.2,10.9,10.6,10.3,9.8]},{name:"Goal",values:[10,10,10,10,10,10,10,10,10,10,10,10]}],yLabel:"Repeat calls (%)",xLabel:"Month"},bookRef:"Book pp. 231, 577–578"}];export{e as items};
